            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "phosphor-power-sequencer/src/chassis.hpp",
                    "line": 274,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "std::expected<bool, string> seems to also be a good option here.  Just mentioning it in case you are interested."
                },
                {
                    "file": "phosphor-power-sequencer/src/chassis.hpp",
                    "line": 274,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "Thank you for telling me about this; I didn't know about the std::expected class.\n\nI think in this case it would be a little confusing. The intent of this method is to return a boolean indicating if the chassis can be set to the specified power state.\n\nIf the answer is true, I would set to value to true and there would be no reason why. So std::expected would work well.\n\nIf the answer is false, then I think I would set the unexpected value to the reason.\n\nSo the caller would have a strange case where false could never be returned, or it would be unclear what to do if false were returned.\n\nI could change it to have a void return value with a string unexpected value, but then the method would probably need to be called verifyCanSetPowerState() or something. That seems less clear.\n\nI'll definitely find uses for std::expected in the future though."
                },
                {
                    "file": "phosphor-power-sequencer/src/power_interface.hpp",
                    "line": 40,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I usually see enums as camelCase.  But, I don't care that much."
                },
                {
                    "file": "phosphor-power-sequencer/src/power_interface.hpp",
                    "line": 40,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "Done."
                }
            ],
